1
00:00:01,620 --> 00:00:04,580
November 24, 1971.

2
00:00:04,730 --> 00:00:07,690
A man buys a plane ticket with a twenty dollar bill.

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:11,640
By midnight, he will hijack a Boeing 727,

4
00:00:11,790 --> 00:00:14,860
collect two hundred thousand dollars in cash,

5
00:00:15,010 --> 00:00:17,950
and jump out of the sky into a freezing storm.

6
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:21,780
The FBI will spend forty-five years hunting him.

7
00:00:21,930 --> 00:00:28,900
They will dig up riverbanks, drain theories, test DNA, and chase more than eight hundred suspects.

8
00:00:29,050 --> 00:00:30,930
They will find nothing.

9
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:35,960
This is the only unsolved airplane hijacking in American history.

10
00:00:36,110 --> 00:00:39,490
This is the case file of D.B. Cooper.

11
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:43,560
Portland International Airport.

12
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:45,030
Two forty-five in the afternoon.

13
00:00:45,030 --> 00:00:51,900
A man in a dark suit, white shirt, and a black clip-on tie walks up to the Northwest Orient counter.

14
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:56,560
He pays twenty dollars cash for a one-way seat to Seattle.

15
00:00:55,780 --> 00:00:57,500
Flight 305.

16
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:00,810
The name he gives is Dan Cooper.

17
00:01:00,030 --> 00:01:02,440
No ID required.

18
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:03,450
It was 1971.

19
00:01:03,450 --> 00:01:06,410
He takes seat 18-E.

20
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:07,810
The very last row of the plane.

21
00:01:07,810 --> 00:01:10,330
He orders a bourbon and soda, and he lights a cigarette.

22
00:01:09,550 --> 00:01:10,940
Just another businessman flying home.

23
00:01:10,940 --> 00:01:13,320
At 2:50 PM, the plane takes off.

24
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:15,970
Thirty-six passengers.

25
00:01:15,190 --> 00:01:17,220
Six crew.

26
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:18,210
A thirty minute hop to Seattle.

27
00:01:18,210 --> 00:01:23,810
Minutes after takeoff, Cooper passes a folded note to the flight attendant, Florence Schaffner.

28
00:01:23,030 --> 00:01:25,900
She is twenty-three.

29
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:27,480
She assumes it is a phone number.

30
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:28,720
This happens all the time.

31
00:01:27,940 --> 00:01:29,950
She drops it in her purse, unread.

32
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:31,710
Cooper leans in.

33
00:01:30,930 --> 00:01:34,960
Miss, you'd better look at that note.

34
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:36,130
I have a bomb.

35
00:01:36,130 --> 00:01:38,400
She opens the note.

36
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:41,790
Neat, felt-pen handwriting.

37
00:01:41,010 --> 00:01:44,060
I have a bomb in my briefcase.

38
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:45,890
I want you to sit by me.

39
00:01:45,890 --> 00:01:48,080
She sits.

40
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:49,100
She asks to see it.

41
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:53,390
Cooper opens the briefcase, just enough.

42
00:01:52,610 --> 00:01:54,440
Red cylinders.

43
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:55,960
Wires.

44
00:01:55,180 --> 00:01:56,300
A large battery.

45
00:01:56,300 --> 00:01:58,060
Then he closes it.

46
00:01:57,280 --> 00:02:01,100
And he starts dictating.

47
00:02:01,750 --> 00:02:03,760
The demands are specific.

48
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:08,010
Two hundred thousand dollars in cash, by five PM.

49
00:02:07,230 --> 00:02:09,190
In a knapsack.

50
00:02:08,410 --> 00:02:10,650
Four parachutes.

51
00:02:09,870 --> 00:02:10,960
Two front, two back.

52
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:15,090
And a fuel truck standing by in Seattle, ready to refuel the plane.

53
00:02:14,310 --> 00:02:17,440
No funny stuff, or I'll do the job.

54
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:20,600
The pilots radio Seattle.

55
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:23,480
And here, the story takes its first strange turn.

56
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:29,880
The president of Northwest Orient, Donald Nyrop, gets on the line and gives one order.

57
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:31,920
We'll show full compliance with Cooper.

58
00:02:31,140 --> 00:02:34,220
Pay him.

59
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:34,340
Whatever he wants.

60
00:02:34,340 --> 00:02:40,380
For the airline, two hundred thousand dollars was cheaper than a destroyed 727.

61
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:42,910
On the ground, the FBI scrambles.

62
00:02:42,130 --> 00:02:44,450
The money is pulled from a Seattle bank.

63
00:02:43,670 --> 00:02:46,070
Ten thousand twenty-dollar bills.

64
00:02:46,070 --> 00:02:54,280
And before the bag is handed over, agents run every single bill through a microfilm camera.

65
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:56,310
Ten thousand serial numbers, photographed.

66
00:02:55,530 --> 00:02:57,160
Remember that detail.

67
00:02:56,380 --> 00:02:58,060
It decides everything later.

68
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:05,440
Meanwhile, the plane circles Seattle for two hours while the cash is counted.

69
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:06,830
Cooper stays calm.

70
00:03:06,050 --> 00:03:08,400
He orders a second bourbon.

71
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:09,670
And he pays for it.

72
00:03:08,890 --> 00:03:10,810
He even tries to tip the crew.

73
00:03:10,810 --> 00:03:13,460
At 5:39 PM, flight 305 finally lands.

74
00:03:13,460 --> 00:03:19,590
One flight attendant walks the cash and the parachutes up the rear stairs.

75
00:03:19,590 --> 00:03:23,470
In exchange, all thirty-six passengers walk free.

76
00:03:22,690 --> 00:03:25,510
Most of them never even knew they were hijacked.

77
00:03:25,510 --> 00:03:30,540
Now it is just Cooper, the pilots, and one flight attendant.

78
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:32,100
And the plane takes off again.

79
00:03:32,750 --> 00:03:36,350
Cooper's flight instructions are precise.

80
00:03:35,570 --> 00:03:39,960
And this is where investigators realized, this man knows aircraft.

81
00:03:39,960 --> 00:03:44,660
Fly to Mexico City.

82
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:46,040
Altitude, ten thousand feet.

83
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:47,480
Flaps at fifteen degrees.

84
00:03:46,700 --> 00:03:49,450
Landing gear down.

85
00:03:48,670 --> 00:03:50,850
Speed, as slow as this plane can fly.

86
00:03:50,070 --> 00:03:53,740
And the rear stairs, the aft stairs under the tail, lowered.

87
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:57,360
The crew tells him the plane cannot reach Mexico like that.

88
00:03:56,580 --> 00:04:01,230
Fine, he says.

89
00:04:00,450 --> 00:04:01,360
Refuel in Reno.

90
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:04,160
At 7:36 PM, the 727 climbs into a cold, black rainstorm.

91
00:04:03,380 --> 00:04:05,730
Cooper sends the last flight attendant to the cockpit.

92
00:04:05,730 --> 00:04:13,160
As she leaves, she sees him standing in the aisle, tying something around his waist.

93
00:04:12,380 --> 00:04:13,740
The money bag.

94
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:16,240
8:00 PM.

95
00:04:15,460 --> 00:04:18,120
A warning light blinks on.

96
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:19,520
The aft stairs are open.

97
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:28,030
Cooper is standing on a metal staircase, ten thousand feet over the dark forests of southwest Washington.

98
00:04:28,030 --> 00:04:31,520
Wind screaming past at over two hundred miles per hour.

99
00:04:30,740 --> 00:04:33,580
Freezing rain.

100
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:34,360
Total darkness below.

101
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:36,310
8:13 PM.

102
00:04:35,530 --> 00:04:38,370
The crew feels it.

103
00:04:37,590 --> 00:04:38,760
A sudden bump.

104
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:45,170
The tail of the plane kicks up, then settles.

105
00:04:44,390 --> 00:04:47,990
That bump was Dan Cooper, stepping off into the night.

106
00:04:47,990 --> 00:04:50,430
Two fighter jets were shadowing the 727 that night.

107
00:04:49,650 --> 00:04:52,020
Neither saw a parachute.

108
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:54,010
Neither saw a man.

109
00:04:53,230 --> 00:04:55,600
Radar saw nothing.

110
00:04:55,600 --> 00:04:57,650
When the plane lands in Reno, agents storm the cabin.

111
00:04:56,870 --> 00:04:58,180
Empty.

112
00:04:58,180 --> 00:05:05,220
He left behind a black clip-on tie, eight cigarette butts, and sixty-six fingerprints that matched no one.

113
00:05:05,220 --> 00:05:08,420
Dan Cooper was gone.

114
00:05:09,070 --> 00:05:12,230
Here is something most people get wrong.

115
00:05:11,450 --> 00:05:14,880
D.B. Cooper is not his name.

116
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:16,030
He never used it.

117
00:05:15,250 --> 00:05:17,570
The ticket said Dan Cooper.

118
00:05:17,570 --> 00:05:20,480
So where did D.B. come from?

119
00:05:19,700 --> 00:05:21,650
A mistake.

120
00:05:20,870 --> 00:05:22,300
Made on deadline.

121
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:28,880
That first night, police checked local records and briefly looked at a Portland man with a criminal record.

122
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:29,690
His name, D.B. Cooper.

123
00:05:28,910 --> 00:05:32,660
He was cleared almost immediately.

124
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:38,740
But a wire reporter named James Long was racing a deadline, and in the rush, the wrong name went out.

125
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:41,120
One newspaper copied it.

126
00:05:40,340 --> 00:05:41,970
Then another.

127
00:05:41,190 --> 00:05:43,460
Then the TV networks.

128
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:46,440
Within days, the whole country was saying D.B. Cooper.

129
00:05:46,440 --> 00:05:50,300
The FBI's own case file never called him that.

130
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:51,150
To them, this was NORJAK.

131
00:05:50,370 --> 00:05:53,110
Northwest hijacking.

132
00:05:53,110 --> 00:05:56,820
But the wrong name had already become a legend.

133
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:58,460
And legends are very hard to kill.

134
00:05:58,460 --> 00:06:00,580
Think about that.

135
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:03,040
Even the name of this man is an error.

136
00:06:02,260 --> 00:06:04,930
The sketch, a guess.

137
00:06:04,150 --> 00:06:06,230
The identity, a blank.

138
00:06:06,230 --> 00:06:14,220
The only hard facts the FBI truly owned were ten thousand serial numbers, sitting in a file, waiting.

139
00:06:14,870 --> 00:06:18,150
The next morning, the hunt begins.

140
00:06:17,370 --> 00:06:22,780
The FBI calculates the jump zone from flight data, wind, and timing.

141
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,210
An area near Ariel, Washington.

142
00:06:24,430 --> 00:06:26,210
Lake Merwin.

143
00:06:25,430 --> 00:06:27,520
Dense pine forest.

144
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:29,930
For eighteen days, hundreds of agents and soldiers comb the woods.

145
00:06:29,150 --> 00:06:33,760
The next spring, the Army sends four hundred troops back in.

146
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:38,070
They search for weeks, on foot, in helicopters, even a submarine in the lake.

147
00:06:38,070 --> 00:06:42,290
They find nothing.

148
00:06:41,510 --> 00:06:43,650
No parachute.

149
00:06:42,870 --> 00:06:44,410
No body.

150
00:06:43,630 --> 00:06:46,020
No money.

151
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:46,350
Not one shred.

152
00:06:46,350 --> 00:06:47,880
So, could anyone even survive that jump?

153
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:49,320
Run the numbers.

154
00:06:49,320 --> 00:06:53,250
Ten thousand feet.

155
00:06:52,470 --> 00:06:54,560
Night.

156
00:06:53,780 --> 00:06:54,970
Freezing rain.

157
00:06:54,190 --> 00:06:58,010
Minus seven Celsius, before wind chill.

158
00:06:57,230 --> 00:07:00,690
Cloud layer at five thousand feet, so he is falling blind.

159
00:06:59,910 --> 00:07:04,610
A reserve chute that was sewn shut, a training dummy.

160
00:07:03,830 --> 00:07:08,450
And below, frozen lakes and hundred-foot trees.

161
00:07:08,450 --> 00:07:10,900
Experts said survival was close to impossible.

162
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:14,970
But here is the twist.

163
00:07:14,190 --> 00:07:17,930
Within months, five copycats hijacked planes and jumped.

164
00:07:17,930 --> 00:07:26,210
One of them, Richard McCoy, jumped from a jet going nearly twice as fast as Cooper's.

165
00:07:25,430 --> 00:07:27,870
McCoy landed, walked away, and went home.

166
00:07:27,870 --> 00:07:29,420
So, impossible?

167
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:31,420
No.

168
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:34,030
Just very, very unlikely.

169
00:07:34,030 --> 00:07:38,440
The FBI was stuck with two open doors.

170
00:07:37,660 --> 00:07:42,130
Door one, Cooper died in those woods, and the forest kept him.

171
00:07:41,350 --> 00:07:45,700
Door two, Cooper walked out, and vanished into America.

172
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:50,280
For nine years, nothing came through either door.

173
00:07:49,500 --> 00:07:53,570
Then a six-year-old boy went camping.

174
00:07:54,220 --> 00:07:56,410
February 10, 1980.

175
00:07:55,630 --> 00:08:00,470
Tena Bar, a sandy riverbank on the Columbia River.

176
00:08:00,470 --> 00:08:05,270
A boy named Brian Ingram is clearing a spot for a campfire.

177
00:08:04,490 --> 00:08:06,900
Three inches under the sand, his hand hits paper.

178
00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:09,090
Three bundles of twenty-dollar bills.

179
00:08:08,310 --> 00:08:12,130
Rotted.

180
00:08:11,350 --> 00:08:13,300
Fused together.

181
00:08:12,520 --> 00:08:14,710
Still wrapped in rubber bands.

182
00:08:13,930 --> 00:08:16,370
Five thousand eight hundred dollars.

183
00:08:16,370 --> 00:08:19,810
The family calls the FBI.

184
00:08:19,030 --> 00:08:21,960
The serial numbers come out of the file.

185
00:08:21,180 --> 00:08:24,210
Match.

186
00:08:23,430 --> 00:08:25,440
Match.

187
00:08:24,660 --> 00:08:25,900
Match.

188
00:08:25,900 --> 00:08:30,450
All five thousand eight hundred dollars, Cooper's ransom.

189
00:08:29,670 --> 00:08:31,270
It is the first physical trace of D.B. Cooper ever found.

190
00:08:31,270 --> 00:08:36,130
And it makes the mystery worse.

191
00:08:35,350 --> 00:08:38,250
Because Tena Bar is on the wrong river.

192
00:08:37,470 --> 00:08:45,540
It sits miles upstream from where Cooper's flight path crosses the water.

193
00:08:44,760 --> 00:08:48,060
Money can float downstream.

194
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:49,830
It cannot swim upstream.

195
00:08:49,830 --> 00:08:52,400
So how did it get there?

196
00:08:51,620 --> 00:08:54,890
Did he land near the river and lose it?

197
00:08:54,110 --> 00:08:56,270
Did he bury it?

198
00:08:55,490 --> 00:08:57,780
Did someone else?

199
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:58,720
Nobody knows.

200
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:04,780
In 2008, Brian Ingram auctioned fifteen of those ruined bills.

201
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,800
They sold for thirty-seven thousand dollars.

202
00:09:07,020 --> 00:09:09,620
Collectors paid triple face value, for money that failed.

203
00:09:09,620 --> 00:09:14,720
And here is the part that keeps investigators awake.

204
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:25,280
Of the remaining one hundred ninety-four thousand two hundred dollars, not one bill, not one single serial number, has ever surfaced.

205
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:26,840
Not in a bank.

206
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:28,350
Not in a casino.

207
00:09:27,570 --> 00:09:30,020
Not in a store.

208
00:09:29,240 --> 00:09:33,690
Fifty-five years of automated serial scanning.

209
00:09:32,910 --> 00:09:34,820
Zero hits.

210
00:09:34,820 --> 00:09:37,990
The money did not just disappear.

211
00:09:37,210 --> 00:09:39,880
It stopped existing.

212
00:09:40,530 --> 00:09:42,620
So who was he?

213
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:44,250
Start with the tie.

214
00:09:43,470 --> 00:09:47,770
The black clip-on tie Cooper left on seat 18-E.

215
00:09:47,770 --> 00:09:52,990
In 2001, the FBI pulled a partial DNA profile off it.

216
00:09:52,210 --> 00:09:53,570
No match.

217
00:09:53,570 --> 00:09:59,060
Then citizen investigators put the tie under an electron microscope.

218
00:09:58,280 --> 00:10:01,340
And found something strange.

219
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:04,120
Particles of pure, unalloyed titanium.

220
00:10:04,120 --> 00:10:09,000
In 1971, almost nobody handled that material.

221
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:10,900
Aerospace plants.

222
00:10:10,120 --> 00:10:13,590
Chemical plants.

223
00:10:12,810 --> 00:10:15,510
Boeing contractors.

224
00:10:15,510 --> 00:10:17,820
Suddenly the profile sharpens.

225
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:20,760
Not a random criminal.

226
00:10:19,980 --> 00:10:22,150
A man from inside the aircraft world.

227
00:10:21,370 --> 00:10:23,220
An engineer.

228
00:10:22,440 --> 00:10:26,510
A manager.

229
00:10:25,730 --> 00:10:28,040
Someone who knew exactly what a 727 could do.

230
00:10:28,040 --> 00:10:32,530
Now, the suspects.

231
00:10:31,750 --> 00:10:33,380
The FBI processed over eight hundred serious candidates.

232
00:10:33,380 --> 00:10:35,550
Suspect number one.

233
00:10:34,770 --> 00:10:36,700
Richard McCoy.

234
00:10:35,920 --> 00:10:40,440
The copycat.

235
00:10:39,660 --> 00:10:44,240
Five months after Cooper, he hijacked a jet with the same method, same demands, and jumped with half a million dollars.

236
00:10:43,460 --> 00:10:45,860
Vietnam veteran.

237
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:47,010
Helicopter pilot.

238
00:10:46,230 --> 00:10:48,220
Skydiver.

239
00:10:47,440 --> 00:10:49,530
Arrested in two days.

240
00:10:49,530 --> 00:10:52,980
McCoy never admitted he was Cooper.

241
00:10:52,200 --> 00:10:57,690
He escaped prison with a fake gun, and died in a shootout with the FBI.

242
00:10:56,910 --> 00:10:59,270
For years, even agents believed he was the answer.

243
00:10:59,270 --> 00:11:03,310
But the FBI says no.

244
00:11:02,530 --> 00:11:10,010
McCoy was in Las Vegas that day, and the flight crew said the face was wrong.

245
00:11:09,230 --> 00:11:10,850
Too young.

246
00:11:10,070 --> 00:11:12,780
Case not closed.

247
00:11:13,430 --> 00:11:15,700
Suspect number two.

248
00:11:14,920 --> 00:11:17,440
Robert Rackstraw.

249
00:11:16,660 --> 00:11:18,660
Army veteran.

250
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:19,390
Pilot.

251
00:11:18,610 --> 00:11:20,920
Explosives training.

252
00:11:20,140 --> 00:11:22,540
A face that matched the sketch.

253
00:11:22,540 --> 00:11:30,900
A forty-person team of retired investigators, led by filmmaker Thomas Colbert, spent years building the case against him.

254
00:11:30,120 --> 00:11:34,220
They even claimed his old letters contained hidden confessions.

255
00:11:34,220 --> 00:11:37,700
The FBI looked at Rackstraw in the seventies.

256
00:11:36,920 --> 00:11:39,020
And dropped him.

257
00:11:38,240 --> 00:11:41,990
He denied everything until he died in 2019.

258
00:11:41,210 --> 00:11:44,400
His life, he said, was ruined by a theory.

259
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:51,320
Then there is Duane Weber, who allegedly whispered on his deathbed, I'm Dan Cooper.

260
00:11:50,540 --> 00:11:53,240
His DNA did not match the tie.

261
00:11:53,240 --> 00:11:59,920
Kenneth Christiansen, a Northwest employee and former paratrooper his own brother suspected.

262
00:11:59,140 --> 00:12:02,960
Sheridan Peterson, a Boeing employee and smokejumper.

263
00:12:02,960 --> 00:12:06,150
Every suspect fits halfway.

264
00:12:05,370 --> 00:12:07,350
Not one fits whole.

265
00:12:07,350 --> 00:12:15,420
On July 8, 2016, after forty-five years, the FBI did something it almost never does.

266
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:18,230
It officially closed an unsolved case.

267
00:12:17,450 --> 00:12:19,410
NORJAK was over.

268
00:12:19,410 --> 00:12:22,680
The evidence went into a vault in Washington.

269
00:12:21,900 --> 00:12:27,030
The file stayed open exactly one inch, for one thing only.

270
00:12:26,250 --> 00:12:28,050
The money.

271
00:12:28,700 --> 00:12:34,580
So, what actually happened on the night of November 24, 1971?

272
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:35,970
You now know everything the FBI knows.

273
00:12:35,190 --> 00:12:37,150
Three possibilities.

274
00:12:37,150 --> 00:12:39,530
A.

275
00:12:38,750 --> 00:12:40,570
Cooper died in the jump.

276
00:12:39,790 --> 00:12:45,090
It explains the silence, and the missing money.

277
00:12:44,310 --> 00:12:47,380
But then, where is the parachute?

278
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:48,920
Where are the bones?

279
00:12:48,140 --> 00:12:52,260
Four hundred troops found nothing.

280
00:12:52,260 --> 00:12:55,620
B.

281
00:12:54,840 --> 00:12:57,770
Cooper survived and got away with it.

282
00:12:56,990 --> 00:13:01,080
It explains the empty forest.

283
00:13:00,300 --> 00:13:03,810
But then, why was the money buried upstream?

284
00:13:03,030 --> 00:13:04,900
And why did he never spend a single bill?

285
00:13:04,900 --> 00:13:07,570
C.

286
00:13:06,790 --> 00:13:08,640
Someone we already met.

287
00:13:07,860 --> 00:13:09,630
McCoy.

288
00:13:08,850 --> 00:13:13,760
Rackstraw.

289
00:13:12,980 --> 00:13:15,290
Someone in those eight hundred files, was Cooper all along.

290
00:13:15,290 --> 00:13:17,240
And the proof died with him.

291
00:13:16,460 --> 00:13:20,880
Pick one.

292
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:23,780
Whichever you choose, you have to ignore one piece of evidence.

293
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:25,910
That is why this case cannot close.

294
00:13:25,130 --> 00:13:27,120
One man.

295
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:29,380
One night.

296
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:31,470
One jump into a storm.

297
00:13:30,690 --> 00:13:33,300
Fifty-five years.

298
00:13:33,300 --> 00:13:35,600
And the only thing we know for certain, is that we know nothing for certain.

299
00:13:35,600 --> 00:13:41,180
The case of D.B. Cooper remains, officially and forever...

300
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:42,410
open.
